### Introduction

A function, type, or class alias doesn't have to be declared before the code that calls or references it -- as long as the declaration exists somewhere else in the same file, referencing it early runs the declaration itself first, then continues as usual. This only applies to declarative things (functions, types, structs, class aliases); a plain variable is the one exception -- see the last section.
###
raft := build_raft()
@assert raft.planks == 6, 'calling a function before its own declaration should still work'

build_raft (; Raft() )

Raft {
	planks := 6
}


### Referencing a type before it's declared

Same idea for types -- constructing one works even if its own `{}` body appears further down the file:
###
survivor := Castaway('Jack')
@assert survivor.name == 'Jack', 'constructing a type before its own declaration should still work'

Castaway {
	name,
	Self ( name; self.name = name )
}


### Class aliases hoist too

`This := That {}` self-declares a class alias -- same declarative category as a bare type declaration, so it hoists the same way. Constructing `Sign()` early works, but calling `.warning()` on it still has to wait until `Warning_Sign`'s own body has actually been reached -- a `.method()` call doesn't hoist the same way a plain identifier reference does:
###
sign := Sign()

Sign := Warning_Sign {}
Warning_Sign {
	warning (; 'Shark sighted' )
}

@assert sign.warning() == 'Shark sighted', 'a class alias should hoist just like the type it declares'


### @load can sit at the bottom of the file too

A top-level `@load` merges its file's own declarations the same declarative way, so code above it can already use whatever it brings in -- handy for keeping your own code up top and imports out of the way at the bottom:
###
sign2 := Div([P('Do not drink the water')])
@assert sign2.to_s() == '<div><p>Do not drink the water</p></div>', 'Div should already be usable, even though backend/html.prog is loaded below'

@load 'frontend/html.prog'


### Variables are the one exception

A plain `:=`/`=` variable assignment is a real step in the program's own order, not a declaration -- reading one before its own line has actually run still raises `Prog::Undeclared_Identifier`, same as any language with top-to-bottom execution:
###
# @puts "`water_level`"     -- raises Prog::Undeclared_Identifier, water_level hasn't run yet
water_level := 12
@assert water_level == 12, "once its own line runs, a plain variable behaves as usual"
